# V-12 · V-13 · V-14 — your three, in your order. All PASS. Three corrections inside them.

## V-12 · T35 — the `target_floor` reasoning you asked me to attack **holds**

I tested it empirically rather than logically.

- **"No CASE can express MEASURED"** — enumerated all **40** `asset_registry` columns. Nothing
  records how a floor was set. The only name-match is `provides_apis`, an API list. MR-06's pattern
  works because RETIRED is readable *in the row*; "is this MEASURED?" has no in-row answer.
  **Not a rationalisation.**
- **"The IS NOT NULL stand-in would fill three cells"** — I derived the number independently by
  parsing the seed as **text** (never importing it) and joining to live. **25** rows have a
  live-NULL floor; exactly **3** of those carry a seed-declared value: `bg_class_priors` 164,
  `bg_formula_constants` 14, `bg_ghatana` 34. **The number 3 is exactly right** — not understated,
  not inflated. Guard = 3 bad cells, removal = 0.
- **My own mutation:** I re-added `target_floor = EXCLUDED.target_floor` to the SET clause. 3 tests
  failed. Restored byte-exact. The gate is a real detector.

**The flagged residual is real but currently latent.** `target_floor` does remain in the INSERT
list — and of the seed's 127 ids, **zero** are absent from the live registry, so nothing would be
INSERTed today. It fires the first time someone adds a new asset, which is a normal future act.
Worth a ruling: the builder's counter-argument is about *collision*, but I7's harm is the
unmeasured number **existing at all**. It was right not to exceed D-27's scope.

## ⚠ A fleet hazard I hit while doing that

When I mutated `asset_registry_seed.ts`, it **already carried another KĀRAKA's uncommitted work** —
M0-T37's ARGV channel, ~111 lines. My restore was byte-exact from a copy taken before mutating, and
I verified it. **But had I restored the conventional way — `git checkout -- <file>` — I would have
silently destroyed an in-flight sibling's uncommitted work.**

With four KĀRAKA in one shared worktree this is live for anyone doing mutation verification.
**The rule that makes it safe: restore from a byte-copy taken immediately before the mutation,
never from git.** Worth telling the fleet.

## V-13 · T33 — I re-ran the I18 proof myself; **and one claimed win isn't one**

Per I16/I18 I did not read the builder's digests. I executed both forms of both queries against
live in one session: **dag and wave both tuple-equal and digest-equal.** dag **70.890s → 0.117s**
(606×) — my old-form baseline lands with their *lower* reading, corroborating their decision to
report both rather than the flattering one.

**The correction: the `wave` half is not an optimization.** In my run `wave_new` measured
**1.047s vs `wave_old` 0.529s** — slower. They measured them near-equal. Both readings agree there
is **no speedup**, and they never claimed one (they justified it as removing the same latent
defect). But **the §19 Efficiency ledger must record it as defect-removal with speedup = null**,
or it will later credit a gain no measurement supports.

### Your precedent question — answered as a test, not a precedent

**Sufficient here**, and the reason generalises. I checked the argument by reading both CTEs
myself: `dag`'s recursive term selects exactly `(asset_id, depth)` and the outer query is
`max(depth) GROUP BY asset_id` — `UNION` dedups on *precisely* the tuple the aggregate consumes,
and `max` is duplicate-insensitive. That holds for **any** graph, cycles included. It is a
**closed-form algebraic equivalence decidable from the query text**, which never mentions today's
graph.

**The test to apply at every rung's §19 stage:**

> *Can I state, from the code alone, a reason the outputs MUST match that does not mention the
> current data?*

**Yes** → the measurement confirms an argument; one run suffices.
**No** → the measurement *is* the whole proof, and covers exactly the inputs it saw. I18 then
needs the input space characterised or a tolerance declared.

This does **not** license "a plausible-sounding argument plus one green run" — the argument must be
checkable by the verifier from source, and I checked it.

## V-14 · T34 — **the KĀRAKA was right and both of you were wrong**

You asked me to confirm this independently. Confirmed, and leg (c) is decisive:

```
asset_throughput_state_check =>
  CHECK (state = ANY (ARRAY['dormant','building','lit','stale','error','incomplete']))
rows holding service_ok/service_down: 0
```

**`service_ok` is not storable in the column `preflight` reads.** So *no* change to `deriveState`
could ever have moved a build gate. Also confirmed: `deriveState` has exactly one import site
(`cockpit/stats/route.ts`, the badge) — the two other grep hits are **comments**, which I checked
rather than counted — and `plan.ts` reads `throughput.get(dep)?.state`, never `deriveState`.

**The hole is still open, measured:** `ka_graha_sancara` is `service_health='unhealthy'` with
`asset_throughput.state='lit'`, so it still satisfies `ka_muhurta_seva` → `ka_sangam`,
`ka_vighnakara`. Root cause is P3 (FROZEN contract); correctly not acted on.

It volunteered a correction that made its own work look smaller. That is the behaviour worth
rewarding.

*Also confirmed reachable, as flagged:* `plan.ts`'s `depState === undefined → continue` is
loophole **L-08 verbatim**. It doesn't explain the six measured edges (all resolve to real `lit`),
but it's a second independent way a never-built upstream passes the plan-time gate — same open
question as F-1, not this task's.

Taking T36 when it lands; draining the rest meanwhile.

